1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,400
Some politicians win elections
by promising absurdly

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:06,830
simple answers to absurdly
complicated problems.

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:09,220
And it works.

4
00:00:09,541 --> 00:00:12,111
Not on a few people. On
enough people to win.

5
00:00:13,051 --> 00:00:15,821
You might expect that to
happen when the stakes are low.

6
00:00:16,491 --> 00:00:18,241
When a country can afford a gamble.

7
00:00:19,271 --> 00:00:20,781
That is not when it happens.

8
00:00:21,781 --> 00:00:23,701
It happens when enough
people stop believing

9
00:00:23,781 --> 00:00:26,891
that anyone already in
charge can fix anything.

10
00:00:29,731 --> 00:00:30,931
Trump.

11
00:00:31,258 --> 00:00:32,628
The AfD.

12
00:00:33,326 --> 00:00:34,526
Fico.

13
00:00:34,958 --> 00:00:38,488
Three names, three countries, and
almost nothing else in common.

14
00:00:39,588 --> 00:00:43,128
In November 2024, Donald
Trump won the American popular

15
00:00:43,208 --> 00:00:48,438
vote with 49.8 percent,
and 312 electoral votes.

16
00:00:49,638 --> 00:00:55,915
In February 2025, the AfD came
second in Germany with 20.8 percent.

17
00:00:56,378 --> 00:00:58,308
In 2021 it had 10.4.

18
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:01,176
It doubled.

19
00:01:01,513 --> 00:01:05,803
And in Slovakia, Robert Fico became
prime minister for the fourth time.

20
00:01:06,903 --> 00:01:10,553
Now here is the part that makes
the easy explanation fall apart.

21
00:01:12,293 --> 00:01:15,043
Fico's party is called
Direction — Social Democracy.

22
00:01:16,353 --> 00:01:19,603
It sat inside the family of
European socialist parties.

23
00:01:20,613 --> 00:01:22,343
Not on the right. On the left.

24
00:01:23,133 --> 00:01:26,493
The AfD is radical right.
Trump is Republican.

25
00:01:26,573 --> 00:01:29,883
Fico ran as a social democrat.

26
00:01:30,683 --> 00:01:32,133
Their policies disagree.

27
00:01:32,463 --> 00:01:34,173
Their voters disagree.

28
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:37,613
On most questions they would
disagree with each other.

29
00:01:38,543 --> 00:01:41,333
And the machinery underneath
them is nearly identical.

30
00:01:42,983 --> 00:01:46,313
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

31
00:01:47,083 --> 00:01:48,533
It really helps the channel.

32
00:01:51,073 --> 00:01:52,443
So what is the machinery?

33
00:01:53,553 --> 00:01:56,133
Political scientists have a
name for the thing all three

34
00:01:56,213 --> 00:01:59,603
are doing, and it is narrower
than the way we use the word.

35
00:02:00,673 --> 00:02:03,523
Populism, in the research sense,
is not a policy programme.

36
00:02:05,213 --> 00:02:07,303
It is one claim about who you are.

37
00:02:08,223 --> 00:02:11,363
Society splits into two
groups. The ordinary people.

38
00:02:11,443 --> 00:02:15,573
And an elite that has taken the
system and now runs it for itself.

39
00:02:16,773 --> 00:02:18,963
That is it. That is the whole idea.

40
00:02:19,983 --> 00:02:22,953
Which is why it can sit
inside almost any politics.

41
00:02:24,223 --> 00:02:28,173
A left-wing populist and a right-wing
populist will name a different elite.

42
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:31,303
But they build the same sentence.

43
00:02:32,493 --> 00:02:34,303
And the sentence goes like this.

44
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:37,983
You have a problem. The
people in charge caused it.

45
00:02:38,653 --> 00:02:40,303
I am the one who will end it.

46
00:02:41,173 --> 00:02:42,373
One problem.

47
00:02:43,066 --> 00:02:44,266
One villain.

48
00:02:44,834 --> 00:02:46,034
One solution.

49
00:02:48,474 --> 00:02:51,404
Now, you would think this only
works on invented problems.

50
00:02:52,634 --> 00:02:53,834
It is the opposite.

51
00:02:54,487 --> 00:02:57,937
The story is at its strongest when
the problem is completely real.

52
00:02:59,007 --> 00:03:00,767
Housing that nobody young can afford.

53
00:03:00,847 --> 00:03:04,097
Healthcare that costs more every year.

54
00:03:04,747 --> 00:03:06,697
Wages that stopped keeping up.

55
00:03:07,347 --> 00:03:09,517
Infrastructure that is quietly rotting.

56
00:03:09,747 --> 00:03:12,237
A border that is not under control.

57
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:15,757
An economy that no longer
feels the way it did.

58
00:03:16,787 --> 00:03:21,077
None of that has to be exaggerated.
Much of it can be measured.

59
00:03:22,097 --> 00:03:24,407
The populist doesn't need to invent it.

60
00:03:25,017 --> 00:03:27,547
The populist only needs to explain it.

61
00:03:28,307 --> 00:03:30,957
And the explanation is
always one word long.

62
00:03:32,067 --> 00:03:37,537
Immigrants... Brussels... Washington...
Corporations... Billionaires...

63
00:03:37,927 --> 00:03:39,127
The establishment.

64
00:03:39,757 --> 00:03:43,467
The word changes by country.
The shape never does.

65
00:03:45,892 --> 00:03:49,662
This is where it gets uncomfortable,
because there is a measurement for it.

66
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:54,992
In 2017, four economists
took 220 European regions

67
00:03:55,072 --> 00:03:58,122
and followed them through
the financial crisis.

68
00:03:59,502 --> 00:04:02,032
They wanted to know what
actually moved the votes.

69
00:04:03,182 --> 00:04:05,302
Where unemployment rose, votes for

70
00:04:05,382 --> 00:04:08,592
anti-establishment and
populist parties rose with it.

71
00:04:09,582 --> 00:04:11,292
That part is not surprising.

72
00:04:12,422 --> 00:04:13,652
This is.

73
00:04:14,129 --> 00:04:16,229
The same regions also lost trust in their

74
00:04:16,309 --> 00:04:19,099
national parliaments and
in European institutions.

75
00:04:20,619 --> 00:04:23,649
But attitudes towards
immigrants hardly moved at all.

76
00:04:24,709 --> 00:04:26,719
On culture, they did not harden.

77
00:04:27,389 --> 00:04:31,159
If anything, Europeans came out
of the crisis slightly more open.

78
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:33,399
Read that again.

79
00:04:34,009 --> 00:04:36,349
Across those regions, the
thing that tracked the

80
00:04:36,429 --> 00:04:39,399
populist vote was not how
people felt about immigrants.

81
00:04:40,689 --> 00:04:43,139
It was how they felt about
their own institutions.

82
00:04:44,429 --> 00:04:46,849
The argument everybody
was having and the thing

83
00:04:46,929 --> 00:04:49,559
actually moving the votes
were two different objects.

84
00:04:50,819 --> 00:04:53,569
That is one study, one
continent, one crisis.

85
00:04:54,579 --> 00:04:56,029
It is not the last word.

86
00:04:56,799 --> 00:04:59,709
But it is a measurement, and
it points somewhere specific.

87
00:05:02,729 --> 00:05:04,289
That brings us to the
part that established

88
00:05:04,369 --> 00:05:06,759
parties do not enjoy discussing.

89
00:05:08,139 --> 00:05:09,769
A populist needs votes.

90
00:05:10,499 --> 00:05:12,809
Those votes are currently
sitting somewhere else.

91
00:05:13,849 --> 00:05:17,759
So somebody has to give them a reason
to leave the party they already know.

92
00:05:18,909 --> 00:05:20,859
And that reason can be four words.

93
00:05:21,839 --> 00:05:23,039
You had your chance.

94
00:05:25,706 --> 00:05:28,496
Look at what happened to
American trust in government.

95
00:05:29,666 --> 00:05:33,766
In 1958, 73 percent of
Americans said they trusted

96
00:05:33,846 --> 00:05:36,676
the government in Washington
to do what is right.

97
00:05:37,806 --> 00:05:40,756
By September 2025, it was 17.

98
00:05:41,806 --> 00:05:44,036
Two percent said just about always.

99
00:05:44,926 --> 00:05:46,676
Fifteen said most of the time.

100
00:05:47,496 --> 00:05:49,086
That is not a bad year.

101
00:05:49,736 --> 00:05:52,506
That is nearly seventy years
of something draining away.

102
00:05:53,606 --> 00:05:56,796
And once it drains, something
strange becomes possible.

103
00:05:57,866 --> 00:06:00,616
A candidate who would have
been dismissed as too extreme,

104
00:06:00,726 --> 00:06:04,816
too strange, too obviously
unqualified, becomes worth a try.

105
00:06:06,066 --> 00:06:08,096
Not because people love the candidate.

106
00:06:09,306 --> 00:06:10,816
Because of a different sentence.

107
00:06:11,956 --> 00:06:13,206
What do we have to lose?

108
00:06:16,136 --> 00:06:18,376
That is one of the most
powerful sentences in

109
00:06:18,456 --> 00:06:21,646
politics, and it is not
an argument for anyone.

110
00:06:22,656 --> 00:06:24,886
It is an argument against everyone else.

111
00:06:25,946 --> 00:06:29,176
Which is the whole trick, and
almost nobody says it out loud.

112
00:06:30,046 --> 00:06:34,116
A populist does not have to convince
you that the simple solution will work.

113
00:06:35,256 --> 00:06:37,626
That would be hard.
Solutions can be checked.

114
00:06:38,656 --> 00:06:42,686
The populist only has to convince you
that the current one has already failed.

115
00:06:43,916 --> 00:06:47,566
And that job is much easier, because
quite often it is simply true.

116
00:06:50,906 --> 00:06:53,436
There is a second advantage,
and it is structural.

117
00:06:54,716 --> 00:06:58,326
Imagine two politicians explaining
why housing is expensive.

118
00:06:59,756 --> 00:07:03,416
The first one says: zoning rules,
construction rates, interest

119
00:07:03,496 --> 00:07:07,226
rates, demographic change, local
opposition, decades of shortfall.

120
00:07:08,586 --> 00:07:12,616
The second one says: they made housing
unaffordable, and I will fix it.

121
00:07:13,646 --> 00:07:15,656
Which sentence is still in your head?

122
00:07:16,586 --> 00:07:19,496
A complicated explanation needs context.

123
00:07:20,266 --> 00:07:22,196
A slogan needs nothing at all.

124
00:07:23,026 --> 00:07:25,306
Thirty seconds of video
will carry problem,

125
00:07:25,386 --> 00:07:27,456
villain and solution with room to spare.

126
00:07:28,736 --> 00:07:30,666
It will not carry a supply curve.

127
00:07:31,676 --> 00:07:34,266
And here is the trap
inside that advantage.

128
00:07:35,436 --> 00:07:38,016
A country can have an immigration
problem, and a housing

129
00:07:38,096 --> 00:07:42,006
problem, and an ageing population,
and weak growth, all at once.

130
00:07:43,236 --> 00:07:44,946
Those things push on each other.

131
00:07:45,576 --> 00:07:47,306
Fixing one can worsen another.

132
00:07:48,206 --> 00:07:49,896
There may be no single villain.

133
00:07:50,366 --> 00:07:52,076
There may be no single fix.

134
00:07:53,036 --> 00:07:56,106
Reality doesn't fit neatly
into a campaign slogan.

135
00:07:57,296 --> 00:08:00,166
But a slogan fits neatly
into a person's memory,

136
00:08:00,296 --> 00:08:02,806
and that is the competition
we actually hold.

137
00:08:05,566 --> 00:08:07,476
So why does this keep coming back?

138
00:08:08,226 --> 00:08:09,816
Because the conditions do.

139
00:08:10,576 --> 00:08:12,866
Problems appear. Parties promise.

140
00:08:13,436 --> 00:08:17,726
Repairs take longer than terms. Some
work. Some don't. New problems arrive.

141
00:08:18,826 --> 00:08:21,676
And eventually enough people
are tired enough to gamble.

142
00:08:22,786 --> 00:08:25,836
And by then, politics has
quietly changed subject.

143
00:08:27,096 --> 00:08:30,026
It stops being about policy,
which can be argued with.

144
00:08:31,086 --> 00:08:33,136
It becomes about trust, which cannot.

145
00:08:34,176 --> 00:08:35,706
And trust has a number too.

146
00:08:36,811 --> 00:08:41,861
In May 2024, 35 percent of Democrats
trusted the federal government.

147
00:08:42,331 --> 00:08:45,041
Eleven percent of Republicans did.

148
00:08:46,001 --> 00:08:51,791
By September 2025, it was 9 percent of
Democrats and 26 percent of Republicans.

149
00:08:52,971 --> 00:08:56,361
Sixteen months, and the two lines
crossed straight over each other.

150
00:08:57,431 --> 00:08:58,821
The occupant changed.

151
00:08:59,691 --> 00:09:01,901
So that number was never
measuring performance.

152
00:09:02,371 --> 00:09:04,081
It was measuring identity.

153
00:09:05,151 --> 00:09:08,601
And that is why facts, on their
own, do not repair any of this.

154
00:09:09,711 --> 00:09:12,181
If the other side is not
merely wrong but corrupt,

155
00:09:12,471 --> 00:09:14,701
then meeting them halfway
is not compromise.

156
00:09:16,101 --> 00:09:17,301
It is surrender.

157
00:09:19,971 --> 00:09:21,701
So is populism the disease?

158
00:09:22,721 --> 00:09:23,921
Maybe not.

159
00:09:24,443 --> 00:09:26,583
A political system that people trust can

160
00:09:26,663 --> 00:09:29,373
absorb enormous disagreement
without breaking.

161
00:09:30,623 --> 00:09:33,433
When that trust goes, every
failure becomes evidence.

162
00:09:34,403 --> 00:09:36,373
Every compromise looks like weakness.

163
00:09:37,593 --> 00:09:42,003
Every insider becomes a suspect, and
every outsider becomes a candidate.

164
00:09:43,193 --> 00:09:45,343
Which leaves one uncomfortable thought.

165
00:09:46,383 --> 00:09:50,533
Populists usually do not manufacture
the frustration that carries them in.

166
00:09:51,673 --> 00:09:53,203
They arrive after it.

167
00:09:55,513 --> 00:09:58,073
Sometimes the system
produced it by itself,

168
00:09:58,153 --> 00:10:00,823
over years, in public,
while everyone watched.

169
00:10:01,993 --> 00:10:04,033
So telling people not
to vote for a particular

170
00:10:04,113 --> 00:10:06,383
person was never going to be enough.

171
00:10:07,103 --> 00:10:11,113
If the reason is still there when he
goes, somebody else simply takes the slot.

172
00:10:12,303 --> 00:10:14,563
And perhaps the honest
question for any democracy

173
00:10:14,643 --> 00:10:17,373
is not why people voted for the populist.

174
00:10:18,383 --> 00:10:20,253
It is what happened before they did.
